  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nLight rain will continue steadily this morning before becoming more \nscattered this afternoon. Chances will be around 50-70% for the \nmorning before dropping to around 20-30%  this afternoon and \ntonight. An additional quarter inch or so of rainfall will be \npossible through roughly noon today and mainly across SEMO into west \nKY.\n\nThe trend continues to shift toward a more northern solution with an \napproaching strong upper-level low and surface low Monday night \nthrough Wednesday. Ensemble guidance continues to hint at a better \nchance of seeing substantial rainfall with around a 60-80% chance of \nthe Quad State seeing an inch or more of rain Monday night through \nWednesday evening. PWAT values are forecast to be around 200-300% of \nnormal for this time of year, so very heavy downpours are expected \nTuesday afternoon. Fortunately, the system will move along\nquickly enough to limit the overall threat for flooding. Gusty \nwinds will likely develop from the south as the pressure \ngradient tightens ahead of the approaching cold front. In fact, \nthere is around a 50-70% chance that max wind gusts reach or \nexceed 30 mph across the area on Tuesday. Overall instability \nlooks to be fairly meager with the system with widespread \nrainfall and cloud cover, so trending a bit lower for severe \nweather probabilities across our area. Shear is progged to be\naround 40kts or better, so any increase in instability with\ndewpoints pushing into the 60s could result in at least some\nsevere weather potential Tuesday afternoon. It's worth keeping\nan eye on for now.\n\nConditions dry out for the end of next week with seasonable \ntemperatures expected.",
